# Erdős Problem #99 — ZM / ZS reconciliation note

**Date:** 2026-09-09  
**Repository base reconciled through:** `a8efd00fa7fca0c4a20911e86efd46cb0aadeb9c`  
**Status:** `LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN`

This note reconciles `campaign_boundary_defect_matching_2026-09-09.md` with the ZS report that landed on `main` while the ZM report was being written:

- `target_c_zf_boundary_surplus_collapse_2026-09-09.md` proves
  \[
  q\ge h\ge\delta\ge\lceil D\rceil
  \]
  and
  \[
  \sigma_B\ge\lceil D\rceil-2.
  \]
- It therefore eliminates the old C33 near-minimal boundary branch on the audited ZF route.
- It also produces a linear family of terminal-disjoint strict size-two circular response minors, but does not lower-bound their numerical size.

The ZM theorem is complementary, not superseded. ZM proves that the actual non-diameter **response graph** contains a terminal-disjoint matching `J` with

\[
|J|\ge q/16.
\]

Feeding ZS into ZM improves the diameter-scale form immediately:

\[
\boxed{|J|\ge q/16\ge\lceil D\rceil/16.}
\tag{R.1}
\]

Thus the weaker bound `(ceil(D)+2)/32` in the original ZM report remains true but is no longer the best available consequence once current `main` is included.

Every edge of `J` is a strictly subdiameter terminal pair with positive Kron response conductance, and C64 gives

\[
0<\sum_{ij\in J}c_{ij}
\le\frac12\operatorname{tr}(\Lambda-L_H).
\tag{R.2}
\]

The logical distinction between ZS and ZM matters:

1. ZS supplies many strict circular two-connection minors. Strictness is a determinant/connection statement and can be arbitrarily small numerically.
2. ZM supplies many actual positive off-diameter Kron conductances, terminal-disjoint as pairs.
3. Neither theorem gives a quantitative lower bound on the aggregate conductance in (R.2).

Accordingly the sharpened response-side endpoint is:

> **ZM mass lemma, current-main form.** Prove that every sufficiently large audited C-II/ZF survivor has a ZM matching `J` satisfying
> \[
> |J|\ge\lceil D\rceil/16
> \]
> and
> \[
> \sum_{ij\in J}c_{ij}\ge c/D
> \]
> for some absolute `c>0`, or prove a stronger lower bound.

Since

\[
\varepsilon:=\frac12\operatorname{tr}(\Lambda-L_H),
\]

(R.2) would then give `D epsilon>=c`, excluding the C66/C70/C71 near-zero phase `D epsilon->0`.

A constant lower bound on the matching mass would instead give an absolute trace gap.

No support-count-to-mass implication is being asserted. The known tiny-conductance and near-star regressions still prevent that shortcut. Any proof of the mass lemma must use the actual unit-contact embedding, the normalized budget `sum_C alpha=D^2`, global minimum-diameter geometry, or another source-specific quantitative invariant.

The direct Level I endpoint is unchanged:

\[
\boxed{
 b+\sum_{F\text{ bounded}}(\deg F-4)>2h.
}
\]

ZS narrows the class in which this must be proved to the linear-boundary-surplus phase; ZM independently narrows the response substitute to a quantitative weight problem on an explicit linear matching.
